#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124152 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "71f8b1c9-7856-52fa-8990-a83fda9ef2a9" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Points for a Differential Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "7134a544-e234-51f8-811b-dce26dc2cbec" :question/math-academy-id "e-21849" :question/problem "$$\nx(x-2)(x+1)y'' + x(x-2)y' + \\dfrac{\\sin x}{(x-2)^2}\\, y = 0\n$$\n\nGiven the points $x = -1,$ $x = 0,$ $x = 1,$ and $x = 2,$ indicate whether they are ordinary or singular points of the differential equation above. If they are singular points, indicate whether they are regular or irregular.\n\nClassify using the normalized coefficients after analytically continuing any removable coefficient singularities." :question/worked-solution "Normalize the equation to $y''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0$, where\n\n$$P(x)=\\frac1{x+1},\\qquad Q(x)=\\frac{\\sin x/x}{(x+1)(x-2)^3}.$$\n\nContinue removable coefficient singularities analytically before classifying. The function $\\sin x/x$ extends to the entire function $\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}(-1)^n x^{2n}/(2n+1)!$, with value $1$ at zero. Thus $P(0)=1$ and $Q(0)=-1/8$, and both coefficients are analytic near zero. The point $x=0$ is ordinary. Both coefficients are also analytic at $x=1$, so that point is ordinary.\n\nAt $x=-1$, $P$ has a pole, while $(x+1)P=1$ and $(x+1)^2Q=(x+1)\\sin x/[x(x-2)^3]$ are analytic. Therefore $x=-1$ is regular singular.\n\nAt $x=2$, $(x-2)P$ is analytic, but $(x-2)^2Q=\\sin x/[x(x+1)(x-2)]$ has a pole because $\\sin2\\ne0$. Therefore $x=2$ is irregular singular."}]}
